In task `task-39181cb7`, seven demonstrations were requested in sequence without first recording a concrete competing-hypothesis question for each next request. The examples varied widely: a partial edge bar plus one interior mark; separate edge bars with interior clues; several isolated marks; and two edge segments framing sparse points. Their outputs included vertical or horizontal propagation, changes to color 4 at selected clue cells, and filling large adjacent regions. The evidence did not converge to a single, explicitly tested rule before the next demo request.

This is not a transformation rule and does not establish the eventual task solution. The reusable lesson is process: after each demo, record exact changed and preserved cells/segments and compare against all earlier pairs. Before spending another request, name the remaining ambiguity and the observation that would discriminate the candidates. If no plausible next demo can be predicted to distinguish them, reconsider whether the task uses a more complex selection mechanism or whether the current examples already constrain a usable candidate; do not continue requesting demos reflexively. Keep uncertainty explicit and avoid treating a broad family resemblance (edge bars, sparse marks, output color 4) as a complete rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790682278/attempts/6ce621ab63d7405ba28d9926f3e5a5ba/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/continual-arc-solving/references/uncertain-demo-loop.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/diagonal-ray-case.md", "content": "# Diagonal ray completion: evidence and caution

In this solved instance, the demos showed sparse same-color diagonal seeds on a uniform canvas. The output sometimes extended only selected sides of a diagonal, and different colors could use different slopes/directions. The final 25x16 test had seeds near the top-left and completed three distinct rays while preserving everything else: 9 from the pair at (row 1,col 3) and (2,4) continued up-left to (0,2); 7 from (3,4) and (4,3) continued down-left to (7,0); 6 from (3,5) and (4,6) continued down-right to (13,15). Coordinates are zero-based.

Other demos in this instance included a 6x17 canvas with 2 extending down-left and 9 down-right; a 28x24 canvas with multiple diagonal colors/rays (including one line extending through both sides of its visible seed segment); and a 30x23 canvas where a two-cell 2 diagonal continued down-right to the canvas edge. Therefore do not assume every same-color pair gets a full infinite diagonal, a ray in the seed order's direction, or the same direction as another color. Use demos to infer how this instance selects axes, sides, and endpoints; preserve unrelated clues and change only the cells supported by that demonstrated construction. In cost-sensitive tasks, request a discriminating demo when materially different ray hypotheses remain.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790682278/attempts/6ce621ab63d7405ba28d9926f3e5a5ba/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/continual-arc-solving/references/diagonal-ray-case.md"}
